---
/**
 * The real app, one scroll below the hero.
 *
 * This is one of the six captures written into src/assets/shots by
 * `make screenshots`; the other five stay in `Screens.astro`. It is promoted
 * out of that section for the reason the section itself argues for.
 *
 * `Screens.astro` says the hand-drawn macOS mocks exist "so a reader can check
 * the drawings against it". Left at the sixth section, the thing to check them
 * against arrived four sections AFTER the drawing that most needs it: the hero
 * draws a popover naming `prod/shopify` running and `staging/shopify` short a
 * credential, and a config file pointing at `/s/prod/shopify`. Both of those
 * sentences are about a PROFILE, and this is the pane that shows what one is.
 * It comes first now, so everything after it reads as a diagram of something
 * already seen.
 *
 * `server` rather than any of the other five, and this is the second answer to
 * that question — `running` held the slot first, back when the hero drew two
 * Activity Monitor panels and this pane's job was to photograph the one process
 * those panels argued for. The hero no longer draws that; it draws one file
 * addressed to one profile. The pane that answers a URL with a profile in it is
 * this one: same server, two credential sets, one of them stopped for a reason
 * it names. `running` counts children, which is the argument `Problem.astro`
 * makes further down, and it is back in `Screens.astro` beside it.
 *
 * The image is IMPORTED, not copied. It is the same file `Screens.astro` reads,
 * and `make screenshots` deletes every .png in that directory on every run —
 * parking a duplicate anywhere else would go stale on the next capture. To
 * change what this shows, change the fixture in
 * apps/apple/Bastion/DemoSeed.swift and re-run.
 *
 * `#shot-server` moved here with the figure, and `Screens.astro` no longer
 * answers to it. The `shot-` prefix is what keeps these anchors from colliding
 * with the bare section ids the nav uses.
 */
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import serverShot from "../assets/shots/server.png";
import SectionHeader from "./SectionHeader.astro";
---

<section class="wrap py-[96px]">
  {/* Eyebrow and heading both stay clear of `Screens.astro` further down, which
      owns "The app" over "A window, not a background process." This section is
      the narrower thing — one pane — and saying so in prose here would state
      that section's heading twice on one page. */}
  <SectionHeader
    eyebrow="The window"
    heading="Two profiles, one installed server."
    class="max-w-[640px]"
  >
    <p class="m-0 text-[15.5px] leading-[1.68] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
      The panel above is drawn, and the line in it addresses a profile. This is what a profile is,
      photographed: one installed server, two named credential sets, serving two accounts without
      either knowing about the other.
    </p>
  </SectionHeader>

  {/* No heading in the caption — `SectionHeader` above carries it, and stating
      it twice on one screen reads as a mistake. */}
  <figure id="shot-server" class="reveal m-0 mt-14 scroll-mt-24">
    {/* The main window renders at the full column, and the other captures are
        scaled against it by `plate()` in `Screens.astro`; there is nothing to
        scale against here. */}
    <Picture
      src={serverShot}
      alt="Bastion's Shopify pane: the installed package at 1.4.2, a running prod profile and a staging profile reading “cannot start — missing SHOPIFY_CLIENT_SECRET”, above the server's environment variables with required and secret badges."
      widths={[754, 1160, 2320]}
      sizes="(min-width: 1320px) 1160px, 100vw"
      formats={["avif", "webp"]}
      fallbackFormat="png"
      class="block h-auto w-full rounded-[14px] border border-rule-2 shadow-[0_24px_60px_-34px_rgb(0_0_0/90%)]"
    />
    <figcaption class="mt-5 max-w-[76ch] text-[14.5px] leading-[1.65] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
      prod is running; staging names the one variable it is waiting for and has therefore never
      started — which is the state the popover above draws it in. The environment table underneath is
      the server's own, read from its manifest rather than typed here, and the values behind the
      secret badges are in the Keychain: this pane can set one and cannot show you one.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
